	// Since p is odd (p >= 2, and p is prime, so p=2 or p is odd)
	// For p=2, p-1=1, inv(1) mod 2 = 1
	// For p odd, (-1)^(p-2) = (-1)^(odd-2) = (-1)^(odd) = -1 ≡ p-1 mod p
	// So inv(p-1) mod p = p-1 if p is odd, 1 if p=2

	// Actually, let's just compute it properly
	// We need inv(p-1) mod p
	// Since gcd(p-1, p) = 1, we can use extended Euclidean algorithm
	// Or Fermat's theorem: inv(p-1) = (p-1)^(p-2) mod p

